1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,465
Tonight I'm going to talk to you about that remarkable continent Antarctica -

2
00:00:05,465 --> 00:00:10,020
remote, hostile and at present uninhabited on a permanent basis.

3
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:14,105
For early explorers, it was the ultimate survival contest;

4
00:00:14,105 --> 00:00:19,553
for researchers like me, it remains a place of great intellectual challenge;

5
00:00:19,553 --> 00:00:25,000
while for the modern tourist, it's simply a wilderness of great beauty.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
First, some facts and figures.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,601
Antarctica is a place of extremes. The highest,

8
00:00:31,601 --> 00:00:38,290
coldest and windiest continent and over fifty-eight times the size of the UK.

9
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:43,587
The ice-cap contains almost 70% of the world's fresh water

10
00:00:43,607 --> 00:00:47,921
and 90% of its ice, but with very low snowfall

11
00:00:47,921 --> 00:00:53,700
most of the continent technically falls unbelievably into the category of 'desert'!

12
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,474
Huge icebergs break off the continent each year,

13
00:00:57,474 --> 00:01:01,740
while in winter half the surrounding ocean freezes over

14
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,550
which means its size almost doubles.

15
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:11,264
Research and exploration has been going on in Antarctica for more than two hundred years,

16
00:01:11,264 --> 00:01:14,820
and has involved scientists from many different countries

17
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:17,530
who work together on research stations.

18
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:28,518
Here science and technical support have been integrated in a very cost-effective way-our Antarctic research programme has several summers-only stations

19
00:01:28,518 --> 00:01:35,660
and two all-year-round ones; I was based on one of the all-year-round ones.

20
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:42,400
The research stations are really self-contained communities of about twenty people.

21
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,620
There's living and working space, a kitchen with a huge food store,

22
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:54,580
a small hospital and a well-equipped gym to ensure everyone keeps fit in their spare time.

23
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:57,860
The station generates its own electricity

24
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:01,940
and communicates with the outside world using a satellite link.

25
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:07,290
Our station-Zero One-had some special features.

26
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:13,310
It wasn't built on land but on an ice-shelf, hundreds of metres thick.

27
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:20,470
Supplies were brought to us on large sledges from a ship fifteen kilometres away at the ice edge.

28
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:25,800
Living in the Antarctic hasn't always been so comfortable.

29
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:31,550
Snow build-ups caused enormous problems for four previous stations on the same site,

30
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:35,530
which were buried and finally crushed by the weight.

31
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:42,655
Fortunately no-one was hurt, but these buildings became a huge challenge to architects

32
00:02:42,655 --> 00:02:45,360
who finally came up with a remarkable solution

33
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:50,327
the buildings are placed on platforms

34
00:02:50,327 --> 00:02:58,000
which can be raised above the changing snow level on legs which are extendable.

35
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,160
Food is one of the most important aspects of survival in a polar climate.

36
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:09,462
People living there need to obtain a lot more energy from their food,

37
00:03:09,462 --> 00:03:13,540
both to keep warm and to undertake heavy physical work.

38
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:19,540
Maybe you know that an adult in the UK will probably need about 1

39
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:23,847
700 kilocalories a day on average;

40
00:03:23,847 --> 00:03:29,000
someone in Antarctica will need about 3,500-just over double!

41
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,470
This energy is provided by foods which are high in carbohydrate and fat.

42
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:41,120
Rations for fieldwork present an additional problem.

43
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:44,420
They need to provide maximum energy

44
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:48,690
but they must also be compact and light for easy transport.

45
00:03:48,690 --> 00:03:56,160
Special boxes are prepared, each containing enough food for one person for twenty days.

46
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:04,186
You may be familiar with coffee processed by freeze-drying, which preserves the quality of the food product

47
00:04:04,186 --> 00:04:11,740
while making a large saving in weight-well, this type of presentation is ideal in our situation.

48
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:15,980
It wasn't available to earlier polar explorers

49
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:19,090
whose diet was commonly insufficient for their health.

50
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:28,110
I think that being at the cutting edge of science has a special appeal for everyone working in Antarctica, in whatever capacity.

51
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:32,399
As a marine biologist, my own research was fascinating;

52
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:39,070
but it's perhaps climate change research that is the most crucial field of study.

53
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:45,520
Within this general field, surveying changes in the volume

54
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:46,798
and stability of the ice-cap is vital,

55
00:04:46,798 --> 00:04:53,560
since these may have profound effects on world sea levels and on ocean currents.

56
00:04:53,560 --> 00:05:01,235
A second important area is monitoring the size of the hole in the ozone layer above Antarctica,

57
00:05:01,235 --> 00:05:05,750
since this is an indicator of global ultra-violet radiation levels.

58
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:10,930
Thirdly, bubbles in the ice-sheet itself provide an index of pollution

59
00:05:10,930 --> 00:05:17,994
because frozen inside them are samples of previous atmospheres over the past 500,000 years,

60
00:05:17,994 --> 00:05:26,000
and these provide us with evidence for the effects of such human activities as agriculture and industry.

61
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:34,600
There are an increasing number of opportunities for young people to work for a period in Antarctica

62
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:38,378
Not only as research assistants in projects like mine,

63
00:05:38,378 --> 00:05:42,160
but also in a wide range of junior administrative

64
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:46,300
and technical positions including vacancies for map-makers.

65
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:55,500
I hope that the insights I've provided will encourage you to take up these opportunities in this fascinating continent.
